<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Forms of Government through History</h1><p class="path-tagline">From chiefs and city-states to parliaments and single parties: how peoples have been governed.</p><p class="path-intro">Peoples have been governed in many ways, and the same forms recur across continents and ages. A chiefdom joined villages under a hereditary chief who ruled through kinship, ritual and tribute; city-states from Sumer to Venice governed themselves and their countryside; Athens let its citizens decide in an assembly, and Rome elected its magistrates. Medieval lords and vassals divided power among landholders, early modern kings claimed it whole, and England's Bill of Rights bound the crown by law. The path ends with parliamentary democracy, born in 18th-century Britain, and the one-party state, which began in Soviet Russia after 1918.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Builder</a> · <a href="/start">the Thinker</a> · <a href="/start">the Timekeeper</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/government/chiefdom"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalaniopuu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · c. 5000 BCE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Chiefdom</span><span class="path-step-why">A chiefdom rules through kinship, ritual and tribute rather than officials, a standing army or written law, and arose independently from Mesopotamia to Polynesia.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The chiefdom was the form in which many farming and fishing peoples first lived under permanent rulers: a hereditary chief at the head of ranked kin, fed by tribute and upheld by ritual rather than by officials.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/cahokia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monks-mound.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · 1050 CE – 1350 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Cahokia</span><span class="path-step-why">Cahokia, built from about 1050 opposite present-day St Louis, raised Monks Mound, about 30 metres high and the largest prehistoric earthwork in the Americas.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/government/city-state"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · c. 3500 BCE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">City-state</span><span class="path-step-why">A city-state governs itself and its countryside with its own gods, laws and army, a form found in Sumer, Greece, among the Maya and in medieval Italy.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The city-state, a city that governs itself and its countryside, was among the earliest forms of state and one of the most inventive.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/athenian-democracy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/athenian-democracy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Term · c. 594–338 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Athenian democracy</span><span class="path-step-why">Athens was governed directly: citizens attended the assembly by right and most officials and large juries were chosen by lot, though only free adult men took part.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Athenian democracy was the system of government that developed from around the 6th century BCE in the Greek city-state of Athens and its territory of Attica, open only to free adult male citizens, probably no more than 30 percent of adults.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/roman-republic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roman-senate.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · 509 BCE – 27 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Roman Republic</span><span class="path-step-why">From 509 to 27 BCE Rome was ruled by elected magistrates, Senate and assemblies, until civil wars from Sulla to Caesar and Octavian ended in monarchy under Augustus.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Roman Republic was the system of government of ancient Rome from the expulsion of the kings, traditionally dated to 509 BCE, until the rise of Augustus in the late first century BCE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/government/feudalism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vassal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Profile · c. 800 CE – 1789 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Feudalism</span><span class="path-step-why">Feudalism bound vassals to lords by oaths of homage and fealty in return for fiefs, leaving power divided among landholders until France abolished it in 1789.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Feudalism is a term for systems in which land was granted in return for military service and loyalty, creating hierarchies of lords and vassals.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-xiv.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1638–1715</span><span class="path-step-name">Louis XIV</span><span class="path-step-why">Louis XIV, King of France from 1643 to 1715, reigned at the height of royal absolutism, while his minister Colbert expanded France's colonies, navy and trading companies.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Louis XIV was King of France from 1643 to 1715, whose long reign marked the height of royal absolutism.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/bill-of-rights-1689"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bill-of-rights-1689.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Work · 1689</span><span class="path-step-name">Bill of Rights 1689</span><span class="path-step-why">The Bill of Rights 1689 forbade the crown to suspend laws or levy taxes without Parliament's consent, and remains a cornerstone of the British constitution.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Bill of Rights 1689 is an Act of the English Parliament that set out the terms on which William III and Mary II took the throne after the Glorious Revolution.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/government/parliamentary-democracy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/westminster-system.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · c. 1721 CE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Parliamentary democracy</span><span class="path-step-why">Between the 1720s and the 1830s real executive power in Britain passed from the monarch to a prime minister and cabinet answerable to the House of Commons.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Parliamentary democracy is government by ministers drawn from and answerable to an elected parliament, which can remove them by withdrawing its confidence.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/government/one-party-state"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Profile · c. 1918 CE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">One-party state</span><span class="path-step-why">The one-party state began in Soviet Russia between 1918 and 1921 and spread to Fascist Italy, Nazi Germany and many new states; most became multi-party after 1989–1991.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The one-party state, in which a single party monopolises power, emerged in Soviet Russia after 1917 and became the form of communist, fascist and many post-colonial regimes.</span></span></a></li></ol>
